{{Short description|Foreign relations and international posture of Balisca}}{{Politics of Balisca}}
The '''international policy of Balisca''' encompasses the diplomatic, military, economic, and ideological strategies through which the [[Baliscan Confederation]] engages with the global community of [[Adonia]]. Unlike the neutral posture adopted in the immediate aftermath of the [[Great Adonian War]], contemporary Baliscan foreign policy is characterized by active alignment with the [[Socialist Bloc]] alongside [[Burawa]], adherence to the principles of [[W:Proletarian internationalism|proletarian internationalism]], and sustained opposition to [[W:Neocolonialism|neocolonialism]]. This orientation emerged following the [[Camboriú War]], a three-month armed conflict with the [[Potocsí]] that fundamentally reoriented the Confederation's strategic posture from defensive isolationism to active solidarity with post-colonial and [[W:Socialist state|socialist states]].
 
Successive Baliscan governments have maintained that its cooperative economic model represents a viable alternative to both capitalist [[W:Developmentalism|developmentalism]] and [[W:State socialism|state socialism]], and actively promotes the export of cooperative institutions through training programs, technical assistance, and ideological education.
 
[[W:Economic diplomacy|Economic diplomacy]] constitutes a central pillar of Baliscan international engagement, with the Confederation utilizing trade, developmental assistance, and technological cooperation as instruments for advancing socialist solidarity and reducing dependence on [[W:Globalized capitalism|capitalist economic institutions]]. Balisca maintains an extensive network of preferential trade agreements with periphery nations, characterized by zero-interest financing arrangements, technology transfer without patent restrictions, and cooperative production arrangements designed to build local industrial capacity rather than extracting raw materials for processing in overseas. The [[Cultural Products Agreement]] and related mechanisms facilitate the export of Baliscan fashion, music, and media as forms of soft power capable of influencing global culture without the imposition of military force. The Confederation's distinctive economic model, characterized by worker cooperatives, social ownership of essential industries, and the absence of private banking, is actively promoted as a developmental alternative through the [[Instituto Baliscano de Economía Cooperativa]] and associated training programs.
 
Balisca maintains membership in several multilateral organizations that reflect its dual orientation toward regional integration and global solidarity. The [[Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO) represents the Confederation's primary vehicle for regional economic cooperation, with the organization's headquarters located in [[Concepción]] and the [[COCO Clearing Union]] facilitating multilateral trade settlement among member states outside of capitalist financial control. The [[Adonian Community]] provides a forum for regional dialogue on matters including environmental protection, indigenous rights, and disaster response, with Balisca playing a leading role in advocating for the interests of Abyalan nations against extracontinental interference. The Confederation's alignment with the Socialist Bloc does not preclude participation in broader international forums, but Baliscan diplomats consistently advance positions emphasizing the rights of the Global Periphery, the necessity of climate reparations from industrialized nations, and the illegitimacy of imperialist intervention in sovereign affairs.

